\ Stack scopes: `{ ( a b -- c ) … }`
\
\ A scope walls off the data stack. Code between `{` and `}` sees an
\ empty stack and can reach its inputs only through their declared
\ names; `}` checks the scope left exactly what it promised, drops the
\ inputs, and leaves the outputs in their place.
\
\ Only success paths live here: the driver feeds the whole file to one
\ ff_eval, so the first hard error would silently skip everything after
\ it. Enforcement is covered by 013_scope_err.ff.

\ Named inputs, checked output count.
: max2 { ( a b -- m ) a b > if a else b then } ;
3 7 max2 .
9 4 max2 .

\ Inputs are readable in any order and any number of times — the whole
\ point being that no stack juggling is needed to get at them.
: rev3 { ( a b c -- x y z ) c b a } ;
1 2 3 rev3 . . .

\ Nested scopes. The inner ones reuse `p`/`q` without colliding, because
\ a name is only bound between its own `{` and `}`.
: quad { ( x1 y1 x2 y2 -- q )
    x2 x1 { ( p q -- s ) p q - dup * }
    y2 y1 { ( p q -- s ) p q - dup * }
    + } ;
1 2 4 6 quad .

\ Recursion through a scope: one barrier per active call.
: fact { ( n -- r ) n 1 > if n n 1 - fact * else 1 then } ;
5 fact .
10 fact .

\ Empty input and empty output sides.
: sink { ( a -- ) a . } ;
42 sink
: five { ( -- n ) 5 } ;
five .

\ `-- ...` leaves an unchecked number of cells.
: three { ( a -- ... ) a a a } ;
5 three . . .

\ `( ... -- ... )` inherits the enclosing barrier rather than installing
\ a fresh one, so the scope can still see the caller's cells.
: passthru { ( ... -- ... ) 1 + } ;
41 passthru .

\ A named input shadows a dictionary word of the same spelling for the
\ body of its scope. Names are resolved before the dictionary and never
\ create an entry, so this is a purely lexical rebinding.
: shadow { ( dup -- n ) dup } ;
7 shadow .

\ A scope may sit inside a definition rather than wrap it.
: mixed { ( a -- b ) a 2 * } ;
: outer 100 mixed + ;
5 outer .

\ CATCH restores the barrier when a THROW unwinds out of an open scope;
\ later scopes must still work.
: boom { ( a -- b ) a 42 throw } ;
1 ' boom catch .
9 mixed .
